# Exports and throwaway fixtures dropped at the repo root. Root-anchored so that
# real fixtures under src/**/__fixtures__/ stay committable. A CSV export of the
# LIVE dataset is real financial data — this rule is a safety net, not tidiness.
/*.csv
# Same net, same reasoning, added after a 650 KB SMIDA feed dump (`smf.xml`) was
# found sitting unignored at the root of a PUBLIC repo — one `git add -A` from
# being published. The two backup names the app itself writes are covered by name rather
# than by extension: a bare `/*.json` would also swallow a new root config.
/*.xml
/quirenote-backup-*.json
/quirenote-before-import-*.json

# Built by the deploy workflow; never committed.
infra/dist/
infra/.aws-sam/

# `nul` is a reserved Windows DEVICE name, and a tool that redirects to it the
# Windows way (`> nul`) while running under Git Bash creates a real file instead
# of discarding output. Git then cannot mmap it and `git add` dies with
# `fatal: mmap failed: Invalid argument` — a message that names nothing useful.
# Happened twice in one session from tooling output. Ignoring it keeps the
# failure from reaching git at all.
nul

# EXCLUDE THE CONTENTS, THEN NEGATE THE SHARED FOUR. `.claude/` holds two kinds of
# thing and they need opposite treatment.
#
# Scratch, the larger part: `worktrees/<name>/` is a SECOND CHECKOUT of this repository
# nested inside it, and every tool that walks the tree followed it in — `git add -A`
# staged two embedded repositories, eslint linted 456 files of 685, vitest collected
# 225 test files against a real 75, prettier reddened `format:check`. Beside it sit
# `settings.local.json` (personal, and this repo is PUBLIC), `todos/`, and whatever
# `/loop` and `/schedule` keep. None of it is ours to track, and because prettier
# rides on this file, anything left unignored also reddens `format:check` on a file
# nobody edited.
#
# Shared, and committed: `settings.json`, `agents/`, `commands/`, `skills/`. An
# intermediate rule hid these too, so `/update-config` would write a permission
# allowlist and `git add -A` would skip it in silence. `src/nested-checkouts.test.ts`
# asserts both directions — the four stay VISIBLE, the rest stay hidden.
#
# THE FORM IS THREE DECISIONS, each measured:
#   `/*` not `/`  — git cannot re-include a file whose parent DIRECTORY is excluded,
#                   so a bare `.claude/` defeats every negation below it in silence.
#   `**/`         — a pattern containing a slash is ROOT-ANCHORED, so `.claude/*`
#                   alone leaks `infra/.claude/…` while eslint and vitest match at
#                   any depth.
#   `!/…`         — the negations ARE root-anchored, deliberately: a nested `.claude/`
#                   is pure session state with no shared half to rescue.
#
# AND THE TOOLS ARE DELIBERATELY ASYMMETRIC HERE. Git commits the shared four;
# eslint and vitest exclude `.claude` WHOLE, because a vendored skill or agent is
# configuration, not this repository's source. Measured: a `*.test.ts` shipped
# under `.claude/skills/` was collected into `pnpm test`. Prettier follows git, so
# `.claude/` needs a `.prettierignore` line of its own — `/update-config`
# writes it with `JSON.stringify(obj, null, 2)`, which emits no trailing newline and
# reddens `format:check` on a file nobody edited — and a skill ships `scripts/*.js`,
# `references/*.json` and `*.yaml` beside its Markdown, four RED in all. Measured.
**/.claude/*
!/.claude/settings.json
!/.claude/agents/
!/.claude/commands/
!/.claude/skills/

# `.prettierignore` carries `.claude/` for the same reason eslint and vitest do: the
# negated entries are NOT ignored here, so prettier reads them and would be the only
# gate that does — failing on files nobody edited.
#
# THE NEGATION LIST IS AN ALLOWLIST, and that is a knowing tradeoff: anything Claude
# Code adds beside those four — `hooks/`, `plugins/`, `output-styles/`,
# `statusline.sh` — is untracked without a word. The concrete case is a project hook
# script committed by reference from `settings.json` while `git add -A` skips the
# script itself. Extend the list deliberately when that happens; do NOT assume
# `.claude/` is fully handled. The same shape means a skill vendored by `git clone`
# into `.claude/skills/<name>` puts a nested `.git` back in reach of `git add -A`. Vitest and eslint do NOT honour it, so
# they are handled in their own configs; `src/nested-checkouts.test.ts` holds all four
# together and probes each directory at DEPTH as well as at the root.

# Build caches and coverage output. Named here because `SKIP` in
# src/repo-root.ts holds them and `src/nested-checkouts.test.ts` requires
# all three tools to agree with that list. `coverage/` is not hypothetical: one
# `vitest --coverage` run leaves an untracked directory and a red `format:check` on
# generated HTML — the exact failure D109 exists to stop, and it was covered by
# nothing at all until this line.
.vite/
.turbo/
coverage/
